>> title: Collard Greens, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: greens, southern, pork, side, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: collard-greens
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: greens, collards, turnip greens, mustard greens, mixed greens, pot likker, potlikker, slow cooker collard greens, crockpot collards
>> pairs-with: ham-hock-stock, skillet-cornbread, hot-water-cornbread, black-eyed-peas
>> servings: 8
>> time: 7 hr 20 min
>> slack: forgiving — greens cooked this way are meant to go well past done; six hours or nine is a difference in colour, not in whether it is dinner, and the pot likker only gets better
>> step.2: trim the ribs out, wash in three waters
>> step.3: render 8 min in a skillet
>> step.4: sweat 6 min
>> step.5: stew on low, 7 hr

Written for low, seven hours, and this is the dish where the machine matches the method instead of shortening it: all day with a hock is how greens are actually cooked, and a pressure cooker is the one buying you something you did not ask for. Two cups of stock, not three quarts — the greens release an astonishing amount of their own and the pot keeps every drop.

Trim @collard greens{4%lb}(1.8 kg; thick ribs pulled out, leaves cut in wide ribbons) and wash them in three changes of water until no grit settles.

Render @thick-cut bacon{6%slices}(chopped) in a #skillet{} for ~render{8%min}. The crock cannot render anything; this is the one pan.

Sweat @&(~1)bacon{} with @yellow onion{1%large}(diced), @garlic{4%cloves}(sliced) for ~sweat{6%min}.

Slow cook @&(~1)pork base{}, @&(~3)washed greens{} with @ham hock stock{2%cup}(475 mL), @cider vinegar{2%Tbs}, @light brown sugar{1%tsp}, @crushed red pepper{1/2%tsp}, @kosher salt{2%tsp}, @black pepper{1%tsp}, @hot sauce{1%Tbs} in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{7%hr}, the greens packed down in handfuls — four pounds fills a six-quart crock to the rim and collapses to a third of that inside an hour. Pour the liquid left in the pot into a cup and send it out beside them; that is pot likker, and the cornbread is for mopping it.
